frost — provenance note
========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`musig2`/`adaptor` modules — self-contained crypto modules not folded
into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

frost — clean-room scaffold from RFC 9591 ("The Flexible Round-Optimized
Schnorr Threshold (FROST) Protocol for Two-Round Schnorr Signatures",
IRTF CFRG, June 2024), **secp256k1/SHA-256 ciphersuite (§6.5) only**,
covering: the prime-order-group/hash-function dependencies (§3), helper
functions — nonce generation (§4.1), polynomials/`derive_interpolating
_value` (§4.2), list operations (§4.3), binding-factor computation
(§4.4), group-commitment computation (§4.5), signature-challenge
computation (§4.6) — the two-round signing protocol (`commit`/§5.1,
`sign`/§5.2, `aggregate`/§5.3, `verify_signature_share`/§5.3), the
secp256k1/SHA-256 ciphersuite instantiation of `H1`..`H5` and
`SerializeElement`/`SerializeScalar` (§6.5), the canonical signature
encoding (Appendix A), `prime_order_sign`/`prime_order_verify` (Appendix
B), and trusted-dealer key generation — `trusted_dealer_keygen`/
`secret_share_shard`/`secret_share_combine`/`polynomial_evaluate`/
`polynomial_interpolate_constant`/`vss_commit` (Appendix C). RFC 9591 is
a public IRTF/IETF specification — not a copyrightable work (merger
doctrine; see CONVENTIONS.md §5) — so no third-party-source NOTICE entry
is strictly required for the spec citation alone. No reference
implementation's source was ported or consulted as a design reference —
this scaffolding pass worked from the RFC 9591 text
(https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9591.txt) and the public API of
`std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1` only (no `ZcashFoundation/frost`, no
`chelseakomlo`/other FROST implementation consulted for algorithm shape
or code).

`H1`/`H2`/`H3`'s `hash_to_field`/`expand_message_xmd` construction
additionally follows RFC 9380 ("Hashing to Elliptic Curves", §5.3
`hash_to_field` and §5.4.1 `expand_message_xmd`) — likewise a public
IETF specification, not a copyrightable work, no reference implementation
consulted; `expandMessageXmd48` (`root.zig`) is a clean-room transcription
of RFC 9380's own pseudocode, specialized to SHA-256/`len_in_bytes = 48`
(this ciphersuite's only value), and cross-validated (`kat_test.zig`)
against RFC 9591's own Appendix E.5 intermediate values (which implicitly
exercise `expand_message_xmd` — see `SPEC.md`).

The RFC 9591 Appendix E.5 "FROST(secp256k1, SHA-256)" test vector
(`kat_vectors.zig`) was transcribed directly from
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9591.txt, Appendix E.5, for this pass.
This is a public specification artifact — the RFC's own published
conformance vector — not copied from any other implementation's test
suite. Only this one vector (of the five ciphersuites RFC 9591 Appendix E
publishes — Ed25519, Ed448, ristretto255, P-256, secp256k1) is embedded;
see `root.zig`'s module doc comment and `SPEC.md` for why the others are
out of scope.

`std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1` (field/scalar arithmetic, `basePoint`,
`mul`/`mulDoubleBasePublic`, `fromSec1`/`toCompressedSec1`,
`scalar.reduce48`/`Scalar.fromBytes48`) and `std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256`
(MIT, part of Zig itself) are used via their public APIs; no new curve or
hash primitive is implemented here. The sibling `bip340` module is listed
as a build dependency by the orchestrator but is NOT used by any function
in this module — see `root.zig`'s explicit BIP340-incompatibility
finding for why FROST(secp256k1, SHA-256) cannot share `bip340`'s
sign/verify/codec logic.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

**Status: scaffold.** Wire codecs, RFC §4.3 list operations, and the five
ciphersuite hash functions (`h1`..`h5`) plus `computeChallenge`/
`nonceGenerate`/`generateNonces` are implemented and cross-validated
byte-exact against RFC 9591 Appendix E.5's own intermediate values. The
ten threshold-specific cryptographic cores (`computeBindingFactors`,
`computeGroupCommitment`, `deriveInterpolatingValue`, `round1Commit`,
`round2Sign`, `aggregate`, `verifySignatureShare`, `verify`,
`trustedDealerKeygen`, `secretShareCombine`) are `@panic("TODO(fable):
...")` stubs with fixed final signatures, awaiting a follow-up crypto
pass. See `SPEC.md`'s "Done record" / "TODO(fable)" tracking.
